# Known invariant violations, measured by TestAdversarialInvariants.
#
# This file is a RATCHET, not an allow-list that silences a test:
#   - an invariant violated HERE and not listed is a NEW VIOLATION -> red
#   - an invariant listed here that now HOLDS is a STALE ENTRY     -> red
#   - each id below was predicted from the source at lane base (see the case's
#     `invariant` field and its detail line in the CI log for the measurement).
#
# Delete a line only when its case holds, so the fix is recorded with its gate.
#
# A FIXED ENTRY LEAVES NO COMMENT BEHIND. Two versions of this file kept the prose of fixed
# violations, which makes a reader believe invariants are still broken when they are being
# measured as held. If an invariant is not listed below, it is not violated.
#
# EMPTY ON 2026-10-25, and here is what became of the thirteen that landed with the ratchet:
#
#   fixed by a change, with a run behind it -- the auto-update trio and the GoTavern-key
#     strictness (P0-C), gzip and redirects and header injection (P0-A), the data-startup lock
#     (the bounded boot), and the relative dataRoot resolved against two roots (the boot now
#     creates the directory the runtime actually uses);
#   corrected against a RECORDED oracle, not "fixed" -- transport.proxy-connect-ignored (the case
#     targeted 127.0.0.1, which Go never proxies, and asserted an environment variable net/http
#     caches on first use), boot.missing-tokenizers (pinned's own catch answers a guesstimate),
#     and this file's last entry boot.legacy-migration-cost (the ten-second pause IS pinned's
#     warning period before user data moves);
#   repaired as harness defects -- a case that measured a credential redaction it had created
#     itself, and one that used a 3s window against a documented 30s budget.
#
# An empty ratchet is not a claim that nothing can go wrong; it is a claim that every invariant in
# internal/adversarial currently holds, which is the only thing this file ever claimed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2026-10-26, one entry added and then REMOVED THE SAME DAY by the change it asked for. The history is
# kept because the REASON it was added is the reason it was possible to fix.
#
# boot.foreign-cwd-relative-data-root   -- ADDED 2026-10-26, REMOVED 2026-10-26 (fixed)
#   The invariant: "the data directory a boot CREATES must be the data directory it USES".
#
#   WHY IT WAS ADDED, AND WHY THAT MATTERED. The line above this section said the relative dataRoot had
#   been fixed because "the boot now creates the directory the runtime actually uses". That was true and
#   INCOMPLETE: the case measuring it planted `dataRoot: "./data"` in a config the boot never parsed (no
#   --configPath, and internal/app/server.go:129-131 reproduces pinned's chdir, so the install-root config
#   was read instead), and its success branch returned okResult without asserting anything. The case was
#   made able to fail FIRST -- explicit --configPath, the assertion-free branch deleted, and a
#   DISCRIMINATING value (./relative-marker-data, a name appearing nowhere else; with `./data` the
#   install-root directory exists either way because `./data` IS the default) -- and only THEN did it
#   report. Measured, run 40444937492 (GoTavern Lane CI, a690270):
#
#     VIOLATED boot.foreign-cwd-relative-data-root
#     observed: the boot created BOTH <work>/relative-marker-data (the launch-directory rendering) and
#     <install>/relative-marker-data (the install-root rendering) of one relative dataRoot
#
#   WHY IT WAS A DEFECT AND NOT A PARITY QUESTION -- CORRECTED. The first entry here said "pinned cannot
#   reach the state: its chdir (server.js:11) happens before the creation (command-line.js:288-297)". That
#   was WRONG about the order and was withdrawn: the creation is INSIDE `parse` (`server.js:8`), which runs
#   BEFORE the chdir at `server.js:11`, so pinned creates the launch-directory rendering and uses the
#   server-directory one too, silently. The defect is therefore not a divergence from pinned -- it is
#   AGENTS 27, which outranks parity: a directory created in whatever folder the user launched from is an
#   unrequested write. The fix and its recorded divergence are ARCHITECTURE 34.5.
#
#   REMOVED WHEN THE CASE HELD. `internal/commandline/paths.go` now creates only the runtime root and
#   reports the discarded rendering as a PathWarning naming both paths; the case's own success branch
#   asserts that exactly one directory exists. Leaving this line would have been a STALE ENTRY red, which
#   is the ratchet working in the other direction.
#
# KEPT AS A WARNING FOR THE NEXT READER: a case that takes a success branch without asserting is invisible
# to this file. The ratchet can only record violations a case can OBSERVE, and both defects above were
# "the case could not see its input" and "the case asserted nothing" -- neither of which is a violation,
# so neither of which this file could have flagged.

